    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d235">

                <pb facs="0388" n="294" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg294"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">790g.00/l</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Baghdad</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Randolph</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Baghdad</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-05-06">May 6, 1929</date>.<lb/>[Received May 28.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 875</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>:</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>The treaty between the United States, Great Britain and Iraq, which has been
                    under negotiation during the last three years, was about to be signed by the
                    Iraq Government just before the resignation of the late Cabinet of Sir Abdul
                    Muhsin Beg al Sa’dun, its provisions being agreeable at that time to the Iraq
                    Government. From statements made to me by the new Prime Minister, during a
                    recent informal conversation, I understand that the provisions in the draft
                    regarding the Capitulations are probably no longer agreeable to the Iraq
                    Government.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Randolph</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
